//! lotus/stereo_ring_mod.rye — ALES177: the RING MODULATOR carried into stereo — ALES75's bipolar-triangle-carrier-on-amplitude run on BOTH channels with the SAME carrier (centre, depth, period), the second rung of the stereo
//! MODULATION class, the tremolo's named sibling. ALES176 opened the class with the stereo tremolo — a triangle LFO that drives the GAIN, held to an ATTENUATION so the carrier never crosses zero and the write's saturate is a proven
//! no-op. The ring modulator is that rung with ONE RESTRICTION LIFTED: the carrier is allowed to cross zero and go negative, flipping the sample's sign; swung fast it multiplies the signal by a bipolar wave and scatters the spectrum
//! into metallic sum-and-difference tones — the ring modulator's unmistakable clang. A ring modulator whose carrier never goes negative is EXACTLY the tremolo; the carrier's sign is the only difference.
//!
//! The crux — the SAME bipolar carrier on BOTH channels is the linked stereo ring modulator: both speakers clang together, the image held. A ring modulator's carrier (centre, depth, period) is scaled units the caller NAMES, not
//! scalars measured across the field, so the same carrier on each channel preserves the stereo image for free — no linking machinery, no measurement. Because each output sample is its own input times the SHARED carrier c(k) at that
//! index, an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical (the same input through the same carrier is the same output), and a PANNED master keeps its pan: both sides multiplied by the same c(k), so the left:right ratio
//! at every sample is untouched — even when c(k) turns negative and flips both signs together, the ratio holds. Like the tremolo and unlike a delay-line modulation, every output depends only on ITS OWN input sample, so a stereo ring
//! modulator writes IN PLACE WITH NO SNAPSHOT — the thinnest modulation discipline, carried whole.
//!
//! The one honest subtlety the tremolo never met carries too: the two's-complement i16 rail is ASYMMETRIC (sample_min = −32768), so a carrier at −unity on a full-negative sample lands +32768, one past sample_max, and ALES75's one
//! true saturate clamps that lone corner to 32767 rather than wrapping — per channel. The carrier's unit magnitude bound holds, so |y| ≤ |x| everywhere but that single corner, on both channels.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadPeriod (a period not a positive multiple of four, or wider than max_clip), BadDepth (a negative depth), BadCarrier (a swing past unity in EITHER direction), and BadRange (a span past the shared
//! length) BEFORE either channel is touched — the carrier and span validated once against the shared length up front (ALES75's own `precheck`, factored out for this), so a refusal never rings one channel and leaves the other torn;
//! then run ALES75's `ring_mod` on each channel with the SAME carrier. A non-negative carrier makes this EXACTLY ALES176's stereo tremolo on both; unity carrier (centre = glide_scale, depth = 0) passes both byte for byte.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO RING LAW: each channel equals ALES75's mono `ring_mod` with the SAME carrier over the same span byte for byte, proven side by side with genuinely different per-channel content — the left rung by
//! the left's samples, the right by the right's, never crossed. THE TREMOLO-SIBLING LAW: a non-negative carrier makes the stereo ring modulator EXACTLY ALES176's stereo tremolo on both channels byte for byte (the two rungs one
//! multiply, the sign the only difference), and a bipolar carrier genuinely differs (a sign the tremolo can never produce). THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master stays identical through the ring modulator (a mono-in-stereo
//! signal never splits), AND a panned pair keeps its left:right ratio at every sample, sign flips and all. THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an all-silent master rings to silence on both; left.len == right.len
//! after; a bad period, a bad depth, a bad carrier, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES177.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-083200_fill-ales177-lotus-stereo-ring-mod.md
//! Stands on: lotus/ring_mod.rye (ALES75) — the mono ring modulator run once per channel, its RingModError reused whole and its `precheck` reused for the up-front atomic validation; lotus/tremolo.rye (ALES74) — the non-negative
//! carrier equality target, carried into stereo at ALES176; lotus/glide.rye (ALES70) — glide_scale, the fixed-point unit (256 = unity); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) —
//! the Clip and the one true saturate that clamps the lone asymmetric corner.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each rung through ALES75's own unit-bounded bipolar carrier (the triangle and multiply in i64, the write saturating once — a no-op except
//! the lone sample_min-inverted corner), siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The carrier is counted in scaled units (256 = unity) and the period in samples, not hertz (a
//! real time base is a later rung). No delay line, no snapshot, no lookahead, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_ring_mod.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const glide = @import("glide.rye");
const ring_mod = @import("ring_mod.rye");
const tremolo = @import("tremolo.rye");

pub const stereo_ring_mod_version = "20260815.083200";

/// The stereo ring modulator's faults are exactly ALES75's own `RingModError` — `BadPeriod`, `BadDepth`, `BadCarrier`, and `BadRange` — reused by name, since carrying the ring modulator into stereo adds no new fault: it validates one
/// shared carrier and span against the shared length, then runs the same mono ring modulator on each channel.
pub const StereoRingModError = ring_mod.RingModError;

/// Apply a ring modulator over `count` samples starting at `start` in BOTH channels of `sc` with the SAME carrier — a bipolar triangle of `depth_scaled` round `centre_scaled` over `period` samples multiplying each sample — by running
/// ALES75's proven mono `ring_mod` on each channel. The shared carrier preserves the stereo image: an identical-channel master stays identical, and a panned master keeps its pan (both sides multiplied by the same carrier, sign flips
/// and all). Validates the carrier and span ONCE against the shared length before either channel is mutated (ALES75's `precheck`): refuses BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadCarrier, and BadRange, both channels untouched. Values only — never
/// the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_ring_mod(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    centre_scaled: i64,
    depth_scaled: i64,
    period: u32,
) StereoRingModError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared carrier preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the carrier and span are legal against the SHARED length — checked before any write, so a refusal never rings one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    try ring_mod.precheck(len, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period);

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels (same carrier, same span, shared length), so each mono ring modulator is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME carrier
    // on each channel, each multiplying its own samples by the shared bipolar wave.
    try ring_mod.ring_mod(&sc.left, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period);
    try ring_mod.ring_mod(&sc.right, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — a ring modulator writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO RING LAW — each channel equals ALES75's mono ring_mod with the SAME carrier byte for byte, different per-channel content; THE TREMOLO-SIBLING LAW — a non-negative carrier equals ALES176's stereo tremolo on
//     both, a bipolar carrier genuinely differing; THE IMAGE LAW — an identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair keeping its ratio through sign flips; THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — silence to
//     silence, balanced, each refusal untouched, count 0 identity

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const scale = glide.glide_scale;

    // 1. THE STEREO RING LAW — each channel equals ALES75's mono ring_mod with the SAME carrier, proven side by side over one span with genuinely different per-channel content: the left rung by the left's samples, the right by the
    //    right's, never crossed.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_ring_mod(&sc, 0, 8, 0, scale, 8); // bipolar: centre 0, depth unity — swings −1 … +1

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &ll);
        try ring_mod.ring_mod(&ml, 0, 8, 0, scale, 8);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &rr);
        try ring_mod.ring_mod(&mr, 0, 8, 0, scale, 8);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftRingNotMono;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightRingNotMono;
        // The two channels genuinely differ — the left is not the right rung, proving no crossing.
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.ChannelsDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 2. THE TREMOLO-SIBLING LAW — a NON-NEGATIVE carrier (centre = depth = scale/2, so the swing 0 … scale never crosses zero) makes the stereo ring modulator EXACTLY ALES176's stereo tremolo on both channels byte for byte; a
    //    bipolar carrier then genuinely differs (a sign the tremolo can never produce).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 1000, -800, 1200, 900, -1100, 700, 1300, -600 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500 };
        const centre: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2);
        const depth: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2);

        var rung = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&rung, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_ring_mod(&rung, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8); // non-negative carrier

        var trem = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&trem, &ll, &rr);
        try tremolo.tremolo(&trem.left, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);
        try tremolo.tremolo(&trem.right, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);
        if (!eq(rung.left.samples(), trem.left.samples())) return error.LeftNonNegNotTremolo;
        if (!eq(rung.right.samples(), trem.right.samples())) return error.RightNonNegNotTremolo;

        // a bipolar carrier (centre 0, depth unity) produces a sign the tremolo cannot — the two rungs genuinely diverge.
        var bip = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bip, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_ring_mod(&bip, 0, 8, 0, scale, 8);
        if (eq(bip.left.samples(), trem.left.samples())) return error.BipolarEqualsTremolo;
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE LAW — an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical through the ring modulator: the same input through the same carrier is the same deterministic output, so the two channels never split.
    {
        const same = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000, 300, -300 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &same, &same);
        try stereo_ring_mod(&sc, 0, 8, 0, scale, 8);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 3b. THE IMAGE LAW, panned through sign flips — a panned pair (the right exactly half the left) keeps its left:right ratio at every sample, because both sides are multiplied by the SAME carrier c(k), even where c(k) turns
    //     negative and flips both signs together. Wherever the pre-ring pair held 2·right == left, the post-ring pair holds it too. The left values are chosen away from the lone sample_min corner so the ratio is exact.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 12000, -4000, 16000, -16000, 2000, -2000 };
        var rr: [8]i16 = undefined;
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 8) : (j += 1) rr[j] = @intCast(@divTrunc(@as(i64, ll[j]), 2)); // right is exactly half the left — a pan
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_ring_mod(&sc, 0, 8, 0, scale, 8); // bipolar — the signs turn
        const lo = sc.left.samples();
        const ro = sc.right.samples();
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < 8) : (k += 1) if (2 * @as(i64, ro[k]) != @as(i64, lo[k])) return error.PanRatioWandered;
    }

    // 4a. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — an all-silent master rings to silence on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_ring_mod(&sc, 0, 4, 0, scale, 4);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000 });
        try stereo_ring_mod(&bc, 0, 4, 0, scale, 4);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 4) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a bad period (not a multiple of four), a bad depth (negative), a bad carrier (swing past unity), and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7, -8 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5, 4 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_ring_mod(&a, 0, 4, 0, scale, 6)) |_| return error.BadPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongBadPeriod;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.PeriodRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_ring_mod(&b, 0, 4, 0, -1, 4)) |_| return error.BadDepthSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDepth) return error.WrongBadDepth; // negative depth
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.DepthRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_ring_mod(&c, 0, 4, scale, scale, 4)) |_| return error.BadCarrierSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCarrier) return error.WrongBadCarrier; // top = scale + scale > scale
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.CarrierRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_ring_mod(&d, 1, 4, 0, scale, 4)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.RangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no sample to ring.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_ring_mod(&noop, 1, 0, 0, scale, 4); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-ring-mod: the RING MODULATOR CARRIED INTO STEREO — ALES75's bipolar-triangle-carrier-on-amplitude run on BOTH channels with the SAME carrier (centre, depth, period), the second rung of the stereo " ++
        "MODULATION class and the tremolo's named sibling. ALES176 opened the class with the stereo tremolo — a triangle LFO on the GAIN held to an ATTENUATION so the carrier never crosses zero; the ring modulator is that rung with " ++
        "ONE RESTRICTION LIFTED: the carrier may cross zero and go negative, flipping the sample's sign and scattering the spectrum into metallic sum-and-difference tones (the clang). A ring modulator whose carrier never goes " ++
        "negative is EXACTLY the tremolo; the carrier's sign is the only difference. The SAME bipolar carrier on both channels is the linked stereo ring modulator: both speakers clang together, the image held. A carrier is scaled " ++
        "units the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same carrier on each channel preserves the image for free; because each output is its own input times the shared carrier c(k), an identical-channel master " ++
        "stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan (both sides multiplied by the same c(k), the left:right ratio untouched even when c(k) turns negative and flips both signs together). Like the tremolo and unlike a " ++
        "delay-line modulation, every output depends only on its own input sample, so it writes in place with no snapshot. The asymmetric i16 rail's lone corner (−32768·−1 = 32768) saturates to 32767 per channel. It validates the " ++
        "carrier and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadCarrier, BadRange; ALES75's precheck reused), so a refusal never rings one channel and leaves the other torn; " ++
        "RingModError reused whole. THE STEREO RING LAW: each channel equals ALES75's mono ring_mod with the same carrier byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing. THE TREMOLO-SIBLING LAW: a non-negative carrier equals " ++
        "ALES176's stereo tremolo on both, a bipolar carrier genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair keeping its left:right ratio through sign flips. THE BALANCE / SILENCE / " ++
        "ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: silence staying silence on both, left.len == right.len after, a bad period / a bad depth / a bad carrier / an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, " ++
        "count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips rung through the proven mono ring modulator's unit-bounded bipolar carrier, the triangle and multiply in i64 and the write saturating once (a no-op except " ++
        "the lone inverted corner), the carrier in scaled units not hertz; no delay line, no snapshot, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
